= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use $\|w\|_{W^{1,\infty}}=\|w\|_\infty+\|w'\|_\infty$. Fix $u$. For $w$ small, all triples $(x,u+tw,u'+tw')$, $0\leq t\leq1$, lie in a fixed <compact set> up to null sets. The continuous derivatives $f_u,f_v$ are uniformly continuous there, with a modulus $\omega(s)\to0$ as $s\to0$. The integral form of the mean-value expansion gives a remainder bounded by
$$
(b-a)\omega(\|w\|_{W^{1,\infty}})(\|w\|_\infty+\|w'\|_\infty).
$$
The proposed linear derivative is bounded because its coefficient <functions> are bounded on that <compact set>. The remainder is little-oh of the <norm>, proving <Fréchet differentiability> and
$$
\boxed{DI(u)w=\int_a^b[f_v(x,u,u')w'+f_u(x,u,u')w]dx.}
$$
The derivative $u'$ only needs to exist almost everywhere; the uniform compact-range bound handles its measurable representative.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $w=t\phi$ into the differentiability expansion. The remainder is $o(|t|\|\phi\|)$, so division by $t$ and passage to zero gives **$g'(0)=DI(u)\phi$**. For $\phi=0$ this is immediate as well.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a zero-trace $\phi$, the curve $u+t\phi$ remains admissible. When $|t|$ is small it is within the weak-minimum neighborhood in $W^{1,\infty}$, so its scalar energy has a minimum at zero. The preceding derivative therefore vanishes. Thus **$DI(u)\phi=0$ for every zero-trace variation**.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <first variation> is
$$
DI(u)\phi=\int_0^1x^2(u'\phi+u\phi')dx=\int_0^1x^2(u\phi)'dx=-\int_0^12xu\phi\,dx.
$$
The endpoint terms vanish. The <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> gives $xu=0$ almost everywhere, hence $u=0$ on $(0,1)$. A Lipschitz representative is then identically zero and cannot have terminal value one. Therefore **there are no admissible solutions of the stationarity equation**.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Stationarity says $\int(2u'\phi'+\phi)=0$, or $2u''=1$ in <distributions>. Integrating gives $u=x^2/4+Cx+D$. The endpoint values force $D=0$ and $C=3/4$. Conversely this polynomial satisfies the first-variation identity for every zero-trace variation. Thus **the only solution is $u(x)=(x^2+3x)/4$**.

= 6
{parent=2007/B}
